- join() method : It stops the current thread until the caller thread is executed.

    Ex:
    main    - is a thread
        (inside main)
        t1.join()
        t2.join()
    -- here, t1 and t2 gets executed and main gets stopped. main will continue until t1 and t2 completed execution.

    Ex: T1 {
            t2.join()
        }
        // here, t2 gets executed and T1 gets stopped. T1 will continue until t2 completes execution.

- When two Threads try to update a same variable at same time , value won't gets updated properly. Sometimes they update the same value. This is known as 'Race Condition'
- To overcome Race condition - we do use 'Synchronized'. We can make a method synchronized. and we can also create a block inside method as synchronized.
- Synchronized :
        1. Only one Thread can access a method at one time
        2. Thread acquires a lock
        3. Slow in operations
        4. Thread safe - only 1 thread can access at one time.
   -- In Banking all debit operations are Thread safe i.e. Synchronized debit operations. It won't allow to debit at same time.
   -- But for Credit operations, it can happen parallelly, so we don't make them Synchronized.

- In Threads, with join() in some scenarios there occurs 'Deadlock'. Ex: t1 waits for t2 and t2 is waiting for t1.

